<h2><strong>Understanding Florida’s Graduated Driver Licensing Program</strong></h2>
<p>The Graduated Driver Licensing (GDL) Program in Florida is a system designed to allow teen drivers to gain driving experience under lower-risk conditions before obtaining full driving privileges. The program involves three stages:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Learner’s License</strong>: Teens must be at least 15 years old to obtain a Learner’s License. During this stage, they are required to complete 50 hours of supervised driving, with 10 of those hours being at night.</li>
<li><strong>Intermediate (Operational) License</strong>: After holding a Learner’s License for at least 12 months without any traffic convictions, teens can progress to the Intermediate License stage.</li>
<li><strong>Full License</strong>: Teens can graduate to a Full License once they have successfully completed the previous stages and met all other requirements.</li>
</ol>
<p>The goal of the GDL program is to incrementally increase the driving privileges of new drivers as they gain experience and maturity, thereby reducing the risk of accidents.</p>
